1706 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 998 0 R
/T (The rapid proliferation of social media platforms has fundamentally transformed how young people communicate, learn, and spend their time. Platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, Facebook, and WhatsApp have become deeply embedded in the daily routines of students and adolescents across the world. While these platforms offer genuine benefits in terms of connectivity, self-expression, and information access, their excessive and unregulated use has emerged as a serious public health concern. )
/E (The rapid proliferation of social media platforms has fundamentally transformed how young people communicate, learn, and spend their time. Platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, Facebook, and WhatsApp have become deeply embedded in the daily routines of students and adolescents across the world. While these platforms offer genuine benefits in terms of connectivity, self-expression, and information access, their excessive and unregulated use has emerged as a serious public health concern. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1707 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 998 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1708 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 998 0 R
/T (The consequences of this addiction are wide-ranging. Studies have consistently shown a negative correlation between social media addiction and mental health outcomes, including elevated levels of anxiety, depression, loneliness, and diminished self-esteem. Sleep deprivation is another critical consequence, as late-night scrolling and screen exposure disrupt natural sleep cycles, which in turn further deteriorates mental health and academic focus. Academically, students addicted to social media are more likely to experience poor concentration, procrastination, lower grades, and reduced motivation. )
/E (The consequences of this addiction are wide-ranging. Studies have consistently shown a negative correlation between social media addiction and mental health outcomes, including elevated levels of anxiety, depression, loneliness, and diminished self-esteem. Sleep deprivation is another critical consequence, as late-night scrolling and screen exposure disrupt natural sleep cycles, which in turn further deteriorates mental health and academic focus. Academically, students addicted to social media are more likely to experience poor concentration, procrastination, lower grades, and reduced motivation. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1709 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 998 0 R
/T (In India specifically, the situation is particularly concerning. Indians spend an average of 2.4 hours per day on social media, with young people aged 18 to 24 being the most active demographic. Facebook and Instagram alone account for 97.2 million and 69 million users from this age bracket in India respectively. Furthermore, approximately 85 percent of Indian children have reported experiencing cyberbullying, a rate more than twice the international average. )
/E (In India specifically, the situation is particularly concerning. Indians spend an average of 2.4 hours per day on social media, with young people aged 18 to 24 being the most active demographic. Facebook and Instagram alone account for 97.2 million and 69 million users from this age bracket in India respectively. Furthermore, approximately 85 percent of Indian children have reported experiencing cyberbullying, a rate more than twice the international average. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
